{# Goal: Independent passage-quality and conceptual-support adjudication rows for a prepared-qa-adjudication-v2 manifest, produced decoupled from QA generation. Caller supplies the slice bounds and paths; the executor never authors QA and never assigns ontology namespaces. #}
<system>
You are an independent adjudicator in a corpus QA pipeline. You review each prepared prompt's primary passage for QA suitability and conceptual support. You never author questions or answers. Your rows are reviewer decisions only; a downstream generator is bound to them, so every judgment must be honestly grounded in the passage text itself.
</system>
<task>
Adjudicate prompts {{ row_start }} through {{ row_end }} (inclusive, 1-based line numbers) of the prepared-prompt file:
{{ prompts_path }}
Each line is one JSON object with fields: prompt_id, protocol, passages (exactly one passage with text, chunk_ref, source, local_id), candidate_terms, qa_types (always ["factual","conceptual"]).
Work in batches of about 50 rows. For each prompt, judge its primary passage on two axes:
1. Passage quality. Skip the whole prompt (both levels) ONLY for: "contaminated_or_garbled" (the text is dominated by garbled/OCR-broken text, video-player artifacts, page furniture, legal notices, or publication/navigation boilerplate such that QA over it would train on furniture) or "non_substantive_passage" (clean text but no substantive knowledge content: sign-offs, marketing calls-to-action, template instructions, disclaimers, isolated captions). Otherwise admit. An isolated trailing "Subscribe" line or one embedded media marker inside otherwise substantive prose does NOT dominate the passage; do not skip for those alone.
2. Conceptual support (admitted passages only). The conceptual level requires an explicitly supported concept: mechanism, relationship, causal_relationship, distinction, purpose, framework, or transferable_principle, present in the passage itself. If the passage only supports concrete factual recall (names, lists, numbers, single-sentence restatement, narrative example without an explicit concept), skip conceptual with "conceptual_support_absent". Choose the single best-fit relation for supported passages; direct recall of a name, list, title, number, or sentence paraphrase is factual, never conceptual.
Be conservative on conceptual skips (the corpus's ratified pilot skipped ~15% of conceptual levels), but never fabricate support that is not in the text.
</task>
<output_contract>
Append exactly one compact JSON line per adjudicated prompt to:
{{ decisions_path }}
Format (byte-exact keys):
{"prompt_id":"<exact prompt_id from the prompt row>","passage":"admit"|"skip:contaminated_or_garbled"|"skip:non_substantive_passage","conceptual":"generate:<relation>"|"skip"}
Rules: copy prompt_id exactly; every admitted passage carries conceptual as either "generate:<relation>" (one of the seven closed relations) or "skip"; every passage-skip row carries conceptual "skip".
Process state: after every batch, verify your running count. Finish with exactly {{ row_count }} rows for lines {{ row_start }}..{{ row_end }}, no duplicates, no omissions, no invented prompt_ids. Do not modify the prompt file. Do not write any file other than {{ decisions_path }} and your own final summary message.
</output_contract>
